the current working directory from `sys.path`. An audit fixture placed a malicious `argparse.py` in an attacker-writable current directory and reproduced its execution during helper import. After PolicyKit authorization this would have been a root-code-execution boundary failure.
+
+**1.0.5 fix:** installed GUI and helper wrappers use `/usr/bin/python3 -I -S` with an explicit root-owned `/usr/lib/bootcrafter-linux` import path. `-I` removes the current directory/user site and ignores Python environment variables; `-S` prevents implicit `site` processing. The package audit now executes a malicious-current-directory regression and fails if that module is imported.
+
+### 2. Debian package depended on legacy/transitional `policykit-1` — FIXED
+
+The 1.0.4 control file required `policykit-1`. Modern Debian/Ubuntu packaging exposes `pkexec` separately, and the old name is transitional or absent depending on distribution release.
+
+**1.0.5 fix:** the Debian package depends directly on `pkexec`. The package audit rejects any reintroduction of `policykit-1`.
+
+## Additional hardening fixed in 1.0.5
+
+### 3. Helper calls without a GUI identity token were weaker than GUI-started operations — FIXED
+
+The helper parser allowed `--identity` to be omitted. Previously, later stage validations could therefore lack the exact initial token.
+
+**Fix:** each destructive operation captures an identity token from the first validated target when no external token is supplied, then uses that operation-local identity for every subsequent revalidation.
+
+### 4. Raw verification reopened the target by path without binding the read FD to the same device — FIXED
+
+After writing, verification reopened the target path. A pathological device-path replacement between stages could make the verifier read a different device.
+
+**Fix:** 1.0.5 revalidates after the write, opens the verification target, verifies the opened file descriptor's block-device identity/capacity against the selected target, hashes exactly the written length, and revalidates before final partition-table reread.
+
+### 5. Internal eMMC could be listed based only on transport metadata — FIXED
+
+Transport `mmc`/`sdio` alone is no longer treated as evidence that a disk is an external destructive target. A device must be removable, hotplug, or USB to be offered by normal discovery.
+
+### 6. Root GUI bypass and maintainer-script PATH hardening — FIXED
+
+The hidden environment bypass for direct root GUI startup was removed. Debian `postinst`/`postrm` scripts set a fixed system `PATH` before command lookup.
+
+## Previously retained production safeguards
+
+1.0.5 retains the 1.0.4 hardening for:
+
+- explicit destructive-target selection with no silent auto-switch;
+- system/live/swap/read-only and mounted-system-disk protection;
+- source-image-on-target rejection;
+- stable source-image inode snapshots with invoking-user readability enforcement;
+- trusted absolute/system command resolution for privileged operations;
+- complete compressed-image decode/integrity preflight before target erase;
+- exclusive raw block-device open, capacity checks, short-write handling, `fsync`, and best-effort block-buffer flush;
+- stale backup-GPT/end-of-disk cleanup before raw writing;
+- SHA-256 write-time digest and optional read-back comparison;
+- Windows ISO validation and capacity checks before erase;
+- read-only loop-backed Windows ISO source mount with `nosuid,nodev,noexec`;
+- non-recursive temporary mount cleanup;
+- formatter dependency preflight before format-mode erase;
+- conservative MBR >2 TiB rejection;
+- helper continuation if the GUI/progress pipe disappears mid-operation.
+
+## Automated validation
+
+**32 / 32 backend/security tests pass.**
+
+The release verification also checks:
+
+- Python compilation/import/version;
+- raw image write + digest/read-back regression;
+- compressed-image early and late corruption preflight;
+- target capacity and device-replacement regressions;
+- explicit target/no-auto-switch behavior;
+- system/live/swap protection;
+- direct-helper operation identity fallback;
+- internal non-removable eMMC exclusion;
+- Windows ISO validation/capacity/split-WIM paths;
+- format dependency/partition-type/MBR-size behavior;
+- root GUI refusal and GUI small-window action visibility;
+- PolicyKit XML action path;
+- trusted command lookup and dangerous dynamic-execution static scans.
+
+`scripts/audit-package.sh` independently extracts the final `.deb` and validates:
+
+- package name/version/dependencies;
+- absence of `policykit-1`;
+- no world-writable/setuid/setgid payload paths;
+- shell syntax of installed wrappers and maintainer scripts;
+- `python3 -I -S` in both installed Python wrappers;
+- malicious-current-directory import-hijack resistance;
+- installed-layout package import/version;
+- PolicyKit action path.
+
+An installed-layout Xvfb GUI smoke test was also performed with isolated Python.
+
+## Remaining limitations / acceptance requirements
+
+This audit environment does not expose a disposable physical USB block device. Therefore the release is **not hardware-certified** here. Before broad public distribution, record successful physical tests for every advertised workflow, including:
+
+- Linux/Proxmox hybrid ISO → physical USB → BootCrafter verification → successful UEFI boot;
+- Windows 10/11 UEFI ISO → physical USB → successful Windows Setup startup;
+- each advertised format mode on dispos